            "id": "g_amrap_timer",
            "slug": "amrap-timer",
            "title": "AMRAP Timer: How to Structure Your CrossFit Workouts",
            "cluster": "fitness",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "AMRAP (As Many Rounds As Possible) workouts use a fixed time window to maximize output. Learn how to set up your timer for every style of AMRAP session.",
            "content": "<h2>What Is an AMRAP Timer?</h2><p>AMRAP stands for <strong>As Many Rounds As Possible</strong>. You set a countdown timer for a fixed duration \u2014 typically 10, 15, or 20 minutes \u2014 and perform as many complete rounds of a circuit as you can before the clock hits zero. The timer is the only rule. This makes AMRAP one of the simplest yet most brutal formats in functional fitness and CrossFit programming.</p><h2>Common AMRAP Durations</h2><p>Choosing the right window depends on the complexity of your movement sequence and your current fitness level:</p><ul><li><strong>5 minutes</strong> \u2014 Short, sprint-style AMRAPs. Usually 2\u20133 movements. Great for finishing a workout.</li><li><strong>10 minutes</strong> \u2014 The classic beginner AMRAP. Enough time to accumulate meaningful volume without complete burnout.</li><li><strong>15 minutes</strong> \u2014 Intermediate athletes. Tests both conditioning and pacing strategy.</li><li><strong>20 minutes</strong> \u2014 Advanced. Requires deliberate pacing from the first minute. Heart rate management is essential.</li><li><strong>30 minutes</strong> \u2014 Endurance AMRAPs. Often seen in competitive CrossFit programming.</li></ul><h2>How to Set Up Your AMRAP Timer</h2><p>An AMRAP timer is simply a countdown timer. Set it to your chosen duration, press start, and go. Unlike interval timers, AMRAPs do not have built-in rest periods \u2014 you manage rest yourself based on how you feel. Many athletes use a simple rule: <em>rest as little as possible while maintaining safe form</em>.</p><h3>Pacing Strategy by Duration</h3><p>For a 10-minute AMRAP, aim to complete rounds at roughly the same speed throughout. If you blow up in minute 3, you chose too heavy a load or too advanced a movement standard. A good rule is to target 80% of your perceived maximum effort for the first half, then push harder in the back half.</p><p>For 20-minute AMRAPs, treat minutes 1\u20135 as a warm-up pace, minutes 6\u201315 as your working pace, and go as hard as you can in the final 5 minutes.</p><h2>Recording Your AMRAP Score</h2><p>Your score is your total rounds plus any additional reps completed. For example, if your circuit has 10 reps per movement and you complete 8 full rounds plus 7 reps, your score is <strong>8+7</strong>. Write this down every time you repeat the workout to track progress over weeks and months.</p><h3>Common AMRAP Workouts and Their Timer Settings</h3><ul><li><strong>Cindy (CrossFit benchmark)</strong> \u2014 20-minute AMRAP of 5 pull-ups, 10 push-ups, 15 air squats</li><li><strong>Death by 10 meters</strong> \u2014 Not traditional AMRAP but uses the same countdown logic</li><li><strong>Mini-AMRAP warmup</strong> \u2014 5-minute AMRAP used as a warm-up before heavy lifting</li></ul><h2>AMRAP vs. EMOM: Which Timer Format Should You Use?</h2><p>AMRAP gives you complete freedom over pacing. EMOM (Every Minute On the Minute) forces you to work within a minute window. If your goal is to develop pacing discipline and conditioning simultaneously, alternate between AMRAPs and EMOMs each week. If you want pure output measurement, AMRAP is the better choice because the score is objective and repeatable.</p><h2>Tips for Getting the Most From an AMRAP Timer</h2><p>Keep your phone or a visible clock nearby so you know how much time remains. Avoid looking at the timer constantly \u2014 check it only at the start of each new round. This prevents psychological pacing errors where athletes slow down when they see time running low. Trust the timer, maintain your rhythm, and push hard in the final 60 seconds.</p>",
