            "id": "g_emom_timer",
            "slug": "emom-timer",
            "title": "EMOM Timer: Every Minute On the Minute Workout Guide",
            "cluster": "fitness",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "EMOM workouts start a new set of reps at the top of every minute, using the remaining time as rest. This guide explains EMOM structures, rep ranges, and timing strategies.",
            "content": "<h2>How an EMOM Timer Works</h2><p>EMOM stands for <strong>Every Minute On the Minute</strong>. At the start of each minute, you perform a prescribed number of reps of one or more exercises. Once your reps are done, whatever time remains in that minute is your rest. The next minute begins automatically, and you repeat.</p><p>The key mechanical element is that your rest is determined by how fast you work. Complete your reps in 30 seconds and you get 30 seconds of rest. Complete them in 50 seconds and you only get 10 seconds of rest. This creates a self-regulating incentive to move efficiently without rushing to the point of poor form.</p><h2>Why Use an EMOM Timer?</h2><p>EMOMs are favored by coaches because they are easy to scale, produce measurable volume, and teach athletes to manage pace under fatigue. They are also extremely flexible \u2014 you can use an EMOM for barbell strength work, bodyweight conditioning, gymnastics skill practice, or even stretching.</p><h2>Common EMOM Structures</h2><h3>Single-Movement EMOM</h3><p>Perform one exercise every minute. Example: 5 heavy deadlifts every minute for 10 minutes. This is a classic strength EMOM. The movement is compound and heavy, and the 10 minutes give you 50 total reps with programmed rest built in.</p><h3>Alternating EMOM (2 Movements)</h3><p>Alternate between two exercises each minute. Minute 1: 10 kettlebell swings. Minute 2: 8 push-ups. Minute 3: repeat. This format is also called an EMOM 2 or Alt-EMOM. It allows one muscle group to recover while another works.</p><h3>3-Movement EMOM</h3><p>Used for conditioning circuits. Minute 1: movement A, Minute 2: movement B, Minute 3: movement C, then repeat. A 15-minute 3-movement EMOM gives you 5 rounds of each exercise.</p><h2>Choosing Rep Counts for EMOMs</h2><p>A well-designed EMOM should take you <strong>30\u201345 seconds</strong> to complete your reps, leaving 15\u201330 seconds of rest. If you are finishing in under 25 seconds, increase reps or load. If you are running over 50 seconds, reduce volume or weight.</p><ul><li>Strength EMOM: 3\u20136 reps at 75\u201385% of max</li><li>Conditioning EMOM: 8\u201315 reps of bodyweight or light load</li><li>Skill EMOM: as many quality reps as possible in 20\u201330 seconds</li></ul><h2>EMOM Duration Guide</h2><ul><li><strong>6 minutes</strong> \u2014 Short strength focus, great as a workout finisher</li><li><strong>10 minutes</strong> \u2014 Classic conditioning EMOM</li><li><strong>15 minutes</strong> \u2014 Intermediate volume with alternating movements</li><li><strong>20 minutes</strong> \u2014 Advanced; requires strong pacing from minute 1</li></ul><h2>Setting Up Your EMOM Timer</h2><p>Set your timer to 1-minute rounds with no rest interval. Each new beep signals the start of the next minute. Some athletes prefer a countdown timer set to total EMOM duration (e.g., 10 minutes) and use the minute markers on the clock instead of an interval timer. Both work equally well.</p>",
