            "id": "g_how_long_plank",
            "slug": "how-long-to-hold-a-plank",
            "title": "How Long Should You Hold a Plank? Beginner to Advanced Guide",
            "cluster": "fitness",
            "primaryHub": "hub_seconds",
            "excerpt": "Plank duration depends on your fitness level and core training goals. This guide gives specific time targets for beginners through advanced athletes with progression timelines.",
            "content": "<h2>The Right Plank Duration for Your Level</h2><p>There is no single correct plank time. The appropriate duration depends on your current fitness level, core training experience, and whether you are using the plank as an endurance test or as one component of a broader core routine. Holding a plank for 2 minutes is not automatically better than 30 seconds if your form breaks down after the first minute.</p><h2>Beginner Plank Times: 15\u201330 Seconds</h2><p>If you are new to planks, start with <strong>3 sets of 15\u201320 seconds</strong> with 60-second rest periods. Focus entirely on form: neutral spine, hips level with shoulders, core engaged, glutes squeezed. Once you can perform 3 sets of 20 seconds with perfect form, increase to 30 seconds per set.</p><p>Progression timeline: most beginners can reach 30 seconds in 2\u20133 weeks of 3x per week practice.</p><h2>Intermediate Plank Times: 30\u201360 Seconds</h2><p>At this stage, the goal is 3 sets of 45\u201360 seconds. Rest 60 seconds between sets. When all three sets feel manageable with no form breakdown, advance to the next level. This range represents functional core endurance for everyday physical demands.</p><h2>Advanced Plank Times: 60\u2013120 Seconds</h2><p>Advanced trainees typically hold planks for <strong>60\u201390 seconds</strong> with 3\u20134 sets. A single 2-minute hold is a reasonable benchmark for well-trained individuals. Beyond 2 minutes, the training stimulus changes from muscular endurance to mental endurance, which has diminishing fitness returns.</p><h2>World Record vs. Practical Training</h2><p>The world record plank hold exceeds 8 hours. This has no practical application for fitness. Research by Stuart McGill (a leading spine biomechanics researcher) suggests that accumulating plank volume through multiple shorter holds produces better core endurance outcomes than a single maximum-duration hold. For example, 10 sets of 10 seconds accumulates 100 seconds of plank time with better motor control throughout than one 90-second hold where form degrades in the final 30 seconds.</p><h2>Plank Timer Progression Schedule</h2><table><tr><td><strong>Week 1\u20132</strong></td><td>3 x 15s with 60s rest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Week 3\u20134</strong></td><td>3 x 25s with 60s rest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Week 5\u20136</strong></td><td>3 x 35s with 45s rest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Week 7\u20138</strong></td><td>3 x 45s with 45s rest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Week 9\u201310</strong></td><td>3 x 60s with 30s rest</td></tr></table><h2>Plank Variation Timer Settings</h2><ul><li><strong>Standard plank</strong>: 30\u201390 seconds based on level</li><li><strong>Side plank</strong>: 20\u201345 seconds per side (harder than front plank)</li><li><strong>RKC plank (maximum tension)</strong>: 10\u201320 seconds per set (very high intensity)</li><li><strong>Plank with shoulder tap</strong>: 30\u201360 seconds (dynamic stability)</li></ul>",
