            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Bread baking is entirely time-dependent, from yeast proofing to the final oven bake. This guide gives specific timing for every stage of the bread-making process.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Timing Is Everything in Bread Baking</h2><p>Unlike most cooking, bread baking cannot be rushed or significantly delayed without affecting the outcome. Yeast activity, gluten development, and Maillard reactions all happen within specific time windows. Understanding and timing each stage produces consistent, professional results at home.</p><h2>Stage 1: Proofing Yeast (5\u201310 Minutes)</h2><p>If using active dry yeast, proof it first: combine yeast with warm water (100\u2013110\u00b0F) and a pinch of sugar. Set a timer for <strong>5\u201310 minutes</strong>. Active yeast will foam and bubble visibly. If nothing happens after 10 minutes, your yeast is dead \u2014 start over with fresh yeast. Instant yeast does not require proofing.</p><h2>Stage 2: Mixing and Initial Rest (10\u201315 Minutes)</h2><p>After combining all ingredients and kneading, the dough needs <strong>10\u201315 minutes</strong> of rest before its primary rise. This rest relaxes the gluten network developed during kneading and makes the dough more extensible.</p><h2>Stage 3: First Rise / Bulk Fermentation (1\u20132 Hours)</h2><p>The first rise is the most variable stage. At room temperature (70\u201375\u00b0F), most bread doughs double in size in <strong>60\u201390 minutes</strong>. At lower temperatures, rises take longer but develop more complex flavor. The dough is ready when it has roughly doubled and a finger poke in the dough rebounds slowly rather than immediately.</p><p>Do not time the first rise to the minute \u2014 time it to the dough's appearance. Use your timer as a check-in, not a final indicator.</p><h2>Stage 4: Shaping and Second Rise (30\u201360 Minutes)</h2><p>After degassing and shaping, the shaped loaf needs a second rise of <strong>30\u201360 minutes</strong> before baking. This rise is shorter because the yeast has already done much of its work. Preheat your oven during this second rise.</p><h2>Stage 5: Baking Time by Bread Type</h2><ul><li><strong>White sandwich loaf</strong>: 25\u201335 minutes at 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C)</li><li><strong>Artisan boule or batard</strong>: 35\u201345 minutes at 450\u00b0F (230\u00b0C)</li><li><strong>Dinner rolls</strong>: 15\u201320 minutes at 375\u00b0F</li><li><strong>Baguettes</strong>: 20\u201325 minutes at 450\u00b0F with steam</li><li><strong>Focaccia</strong>: 20\u201325 minutes at 400\u00b0F (205\u00b0C)</li><li><strong>Sourdough loaf</strong>: 40\u201350 minutes at 500\u00b0F (260\u00b0C) in a Dutch oven</li></ul><h2>The Doneness Test</h2><p>Bread is done when it reaches an internal temperature of <strong>190\u2013210\u00b0F (88\u201399\u00b0C)</strong> depending on the recipe. Use an instant-read thermometer rather than relying solely on appearance. Tap the bottom \u2014 it should sound hollow. Let bread rest for at least 30 minutes before slicing or the interior will be gummy.</p>",
