            "id": "g_coffee_brew_timer",
            "slug": "coffee-brew-timer",
            "title": "Coffee Brew Timer: Exact Timing for Every Brewing Method",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Different coffee brewing methods require precise timing for optimal extraction. Get the exact brew times for pour-over, French press, espresso, cold brew, and more.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Coffee Brew Time Matters</h2><p>Coffee extraction is a time-dependent process. Under-extracted coffee (too short) tastes sour and thin. Over-extracted coffee (too long) tastes bitter and harsh. Every brewing method has an optimal extraction window based on the coffee-to-water ratio, grind size, and water temperature. A timer removes guesswork and makes your best cup repeatable.</p><h2>Brew Times by Method</h2><h3>Espresso: 25\u201330 Seconds</h3><p>A correctly extracted espresso shot pulls in <strong>25\u201330 seconds</strong> from the moment you start the pump. Shorter than 20 seconds indicates under-extraction (too coarse a grind or too high a dose). Longer than 35 seconds indicates over-extraction (too fine a grind or too little dose). Adjust the grind first before changing other variables.</p><h3>Pour-Over (V60, Chemex, Kalita): 3\u20134 Minutes</h3><p>The pour-over brew process has two phases. The bloom: pour twice the weight of grounds in water and wait <strong>30\u201345 seconds</strong> for CO2 to off-gas. Then pour in stages over <strong>2.5\u20133 minutes</strong> for a total brew time of 3\u20134 minutes. Faster means coarser grind; slower means finer.</p><h3>French Press: 4 Minutes</h3><p>Add grounds, pour water, stir, put the lid on (plunger up), and set a timer for <strong>4 minutes</strong>. After 4 minutes, slowly press the plunger. Do not steep longer \u2014 once the plunger is pressed, pour immediately to stop extraction. Leaving coffee in the French press continues extraction through the grounds in the bottom, producing bitter coffee.</p><h3>AeroPress: 1\u20132 Minutes</h3><p>The AeroPress is one of the most flexible brewers. Standard recipe: steep for <strong>60\u201390 seconds</strong>, then press slowly over 20\u201330 seconds. Inverted method allows for longer steeping (2\u20133 minutes) before pressing. Experiment with different steep times to find your preferred flavor profile.</p><h3>Cold Brew: 12\u201324 Hours</h3><p>Cold brew requires an extended steep at room temperature or in the refrigerator. Room temperature: <strong>12\u201315 hours</strong>. Refrigerator: <strong>18\u201324 hours</strong>. Longer extraction produces more caffeine and stronger flavor but can become bitter beyond 24 hours. Strain through a fine filter or cheesecloth and dilute with water or milk.</p><h3>Moka Pot: 4\u20136 Minutes</h3><p>Use medium heat. Water should pass through the grounds in <strong>4\u20136 minutes</strong>. Gurgling sound signals the brew is nearly complete. Remove from heat immediately when the gurgling starts \u2014 continued heat produces bitter, burnt coffee.</p>",
