            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Baking time for chicken varies significantly by cut, size, and oven temperature. This guide gives precise timing for every chicken cut from breasts to whole birds.",
            "content": "<h2>Safe Internal Temperature: Always the Priority</h2><p>Before discussing times, the fundamental rule: chicken is safe to eat when it reaches an internal temperature of <strong>165\u00b0F (74\u00b0C)</strong> at the thickest part. This is the FDA minimum safe temperature. Use an instant-read thermometer inserted away from bone to verify. Time is a guide; temperature is the standard.</p><h2>Baking Times at 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C)</h2><h3>Boneless Skinless Chicken Breasts</h3><ul><li>Small (4\u20135 oz): 20\u201325 minutes</li><li>Medium (6\u20138 oz): 25\u201330 minutes</li><li>Large (9\u201312 oz): 30\u201335 minutes</li></ul><h3>Bone-In Chicken Breasts</h3><ul><li>Small (6\u20138 oz): 35\u201340 minutes</li><li>Large (10\u201312 oz): 40\u201350 minutes</li></ul><h3>Chicken Thighs</h3><ul><li>Boneless skinless: 20\u201325 minutes at 400\u00b0F (205\u00b0C)</li><li>Bone-in, skin-on: 35\u201345 minutes at 425\u00b0F (220\u00b0C)</li></ul><h3>Chicken Drumsticks</h3><ul><li>4\u20136 oz each: 30\u201340 minutes at 400\u00b0F</li></ul><h3>Whole Chicken</h3><ul><li>3\u20134 lbs: 1 hour 20 minutes at 350\u00b0F (175\u00b0C)</li><li>4\u20135 lbs: 1 hour 30\u201345 minutes at 350\u00b0F</li><li>5\u20137 lbs: 2\u20132.5 hours at 350\u00b0F</li></ul><h2>High-Heat vs. Low-and-Slow Baking</h2><p>Baking chicken at <strong>425\u00b0F (220\u00b0C)</strong> produces crispier skin in less time but requires more careful monitoring to prevent drying. A 6-oz boneless breast at 425\u00b0F takes about 20 minutes versus 28 minutes at 375\u00b0F. The higher-heat method is excellent for bone-in, skin-on pieces where browning is desired.</p><p>The <strong>low-and-slow method</strong> (325\u00b0F / 165\u00b0C) takes longer but produces more tender, juicy meat by allowing collagen to break down gradually. Whole chickens benefit from this approach when you have time.</p><h2>Resting Time: Do Not Skip This</h2><p>After removing chicken from the oven, rest it for <strong>5 minutes (breasts) to 10 minutes (whole bird)</strong> before cutting. Resting allows juices to redistribute throughout the meat. Cutting immediately causes most of the moisture to run out, leaving dry chicken.</p><h2>Timer Setup for Baking Chicken</h2><p>Set your oven timer to the minimum suggested time for your cut and weight. Check internal temperature at that point. If the chicken is not at 165\u00b0F, return it to the oven for 5-minute increments and recheck. Over-checking causes heat loss, so only open the oven when your timer signals.</p>",
