            "slug": "how-long-to-boil-eggs",
            "title": "How Long to Boil Eggs? Soft, Medium, and Hard-Boiled Timing Guide",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Boiling eggs is entirely a timing exercise. Get precise times for soft, medium, hard-boiled, and jammy eggs \u2014 plus the ice bath method for easy peeling.",
            "content": "<h2>The Egg Timer Problem</h2><p>Most people have overcooked boiled eggs \u2014 the green ring around the yolk, the rubbery white, the chalky center. These are all symptoms of too much time on heat. Eggs are protein-dense and cook quickly; even 60 seconds too long changes the result significantly.</p><h2>Starting Point: Room Temperature vs. Cold Eggs</h2><p>Eggs taken directly from the refrigerator take 1\u20132 minutes longer than room-temperature eggs. For the most predictable results, either bring eggs to room temperature for 15 minutes before boiling or add 1\u20132 minutes to the times below if cooking straight from cold.</p><h2>The Standard Method (Boiling Water Start)</h2><p>Bring water to a rolling boil, gently lower eggs with a spoon, reduce to a gentle boil, and start your timer.</p><h3>Soft-Boiled Eggs (Runny Yolk)</h3><ul><li>Extra runny yolk: <strong>4 minutes</strong></li><li>Runny yolk: <strong>5 minutes</strong></li><li>Slightly set, creamy yolk: <strong>6 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Jammy Eggs (Partially Set Yolk)</h3><ul><li>Classic ramen egg texture: <strong>6\u20137 minutes</strong></li><li>Fudgy, fully set but not dry: <strong>8 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Hard-Boiled Eggs (Fully Set Yolk)</h3><ul><li>Fully cooked, still moist: <strong>9\u201310 minutes</strong></li><li>Traditional firm hard-boiled: <strong>11\u201312 minutes</strong></li><li>Avoid: 13+ minutes produces the green ring (iron sulfide from overcooked yolk)</li></ul><h2>The Cold Start Method (Steamer or Cold Water)</h2><p>Place eggs in cold water, bring to a boil, reduce heat, and cook:</p><ul><li>Soft-boiled: 7\u20138 minutes from first boil</li><li>Hard-boiled: 10\u201312 minutes from first boil</li></ul><h2>The Ice Bath: Essential for Peeling</h2><p>Immediately after the timer ends, transfer eggs to a bowl of ice water for <strong>5 minutes</strong>. This does three things: stops the cooking immediately, makes peeling dramatically easier, and prevents the green sulfur ring. Do not skip this step if you are hard-boiling eggs for any preparation requiring clean peeling (deviled eggs, egg salad, Ni\u00e7oise salad).</p><h2>Altitude Adjustments</h2><p>At high altitude, water boils at a lower temperature. Add 1\u20132 minutes to all times above if you are above 5,000 feet elevation.</p>",
