            "id": "g_cook_salmon_timer",
            "slug": "how-long-to-cook-salmon",
            "title": "How Long to Cook Salmon? Baked, Grilled, and Pan-Seared Timing",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Salmon cooks quickly and is easily overcooked. This guide gives precise timing for baked, grilled, pan-seared, and poached salmon by thickness and desired doneness.",
            "content": "<h2>The Key Variable: Thickness, Not Weight</h2><p>Salmon cooking time is primarily determined by the thickness of the fillet, not its weight. A thick 8-oz center-cut fillet needs more time than a thinner 8-oz tail piece. Measure the thickest part of the fillet before setting your timer.</p><h2>The General Rule for Salmon</h2><p>The classic guideline is <strong>4\u20136 minutes per half-inch of thickness</strong> when cooking at medium to high heat. A 1-inch fillet cooks in 8\u201312 minutes; a 1.5-inch fillet cooks in 12\u201318 minutes.</p><h2>Baked Salmon Timing</h2><h3>At 400\u00b0F (205\u00b0C)</h3><ul><li>Thin fillet (under 1 inch): 8\u201310 minutes</li><li>Medium fillet (1\u20131.25 inches): 10\u201314 minutes</li><li>Thick center-cut (1.5+ inches): 14\u201318 minutes</li></ul><h3>At 275\u00b0F (135\u00b0C) \u2014 Low and Slow</h3><p>Slow-baked salmon produces exceptionally silky, almost raw-textured flesh throughout. A 1-inch fillet takes <strong>25\u201330 minutes</strong> at this temperature. Internal temperature target: 125\u00b0F (52\u00b0C) for medium-rare, 145\u00b0F (63\u00b0C) for fully cooked.</p><h2>Pan-Seared Salmon Timing</h2><p>Heat a skillet over medium-high heat until very hot. Add oil. Place salmon skin-side up and cook for <strong>3\u20134 minutes</strong> without moving until deep golden and crispy on the bottom. Flip and cook another <strong>2\u20133 minutes</strong> skin-side down for medium. Total time: 5\u20137 minutes for a 1-inch fillet.</p><h2>Grilled Salmon Timing</h2><p>Preheat grill to medium-high (400\u00b0F). Grill skin-side down for <strong>4\u20135 minutes</strong> per side for a 1-inch fillet. Avoid moving the fish \u2014 let it release naturally from the grates. A fish spatula helps with flipping. Total time: 8\u201310 minutes.</p><h2>Doneness by Internal Temperature</h2><ul><li><strong>120\u2013125\u00b0F (49\u201352\u00b0C)</strong>: Medium-rare \u2014 translucent center, very silky</li><li><strong>130\u2013135\u00b0F (54\u201357\u00b0C)</strong>: Medium \u2014 slightly translucent, moist and flaky</li><li><strong>145\u00b0F (63\u00b0C)</strong>: FDA-recommended fully cooked \u2014 opaque throughout, slightly firmer</li></ul><h2>The Knife Test (Without a Thermometer)</h2><p>Insert the tip of a thin knife into the thickest part and hold it there for 2 seconds. Touch the flat of the blade to your lip. If it is cold, the fish is raw. If warm, it is medium-rare. If hot, it is fully cooked. This is a professional kitchen technique for gauging doneness without equipment.</p>",
