            "id": "g_grill_chicken_timer",
            "slug": "how-long-to-grill-chicken",
            "title": "How Long to Grill Chicken? Safe and Perfectly Cooked Every Time",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Grilling chicken requires careful timing to achieve the internal temperature of 165\u00b0F while maintaining juicy, non-rubbery texture. This guide covers timing for every cut.",
            "content": "<h2>Grilling Chicken Safely</h2><p>Chicken is more forgiving on the grill than many people believe, but it requires reaching <strong>165\u00b0F (74\u00b0C)</strong> internal temperature without charring the outside or drying the inside. The keys are correct grill temperature, appropriate technique for each cut, and a reliable timer combined with an instant-read thermometer.</p><h2>Grill Temperature Setup</h2><p>Most chicken cuts grill best over <strong>medium heat (350\u2013400\u00b0F)</strong>. Breasts and thinner cuts benefit from a two-zone setup: sear over high heat, then finish over indirect medium heat. This prevents the outside from burning before the center reaches safe temperature.</p><h2>Grilling Times by Cut</h2><h3>Boneless Skinless Chicken Breasts</h3><p>Medium heat, lid closed: <strong>6\u20138 minutes per side</strong> for a 6\u20138 oz breast. Total: 12\u201316 minutes. Flip only once. Resist the urge to flip repeatedly \u2014 let each side develop grill marks and proper browning.</p><h3>Bone-In Chicken Breasts</h3><p>Sear over direct heat for 2\u20133 minutes per side, then move to indirect heat and cook with lid closed for <strong>20\u201330 minutes</strong>. Total: 25\u201335 minutes.</p><h3>Chicken Thighs (Boneless Skinless)</h3><p>Thighs are more forgiving than breasts due to higher fat content. Grill over medium-high heat: <strong>5\u20137 minutes per side</strong>. Total: 10\u201314 minutes. They benefit from slightly higher heat than breasts because the fat renders better.</p><h3>Chicken Thighs (Bone-In, Skin-On)</h3><p>Sear skin-side down over direct heat for 5 minutes. Flip and move to indirect heat. Cook with lid closed for <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong>. Total: 25\u201330 minutes. Check that skin does not burn over direct heat.</p><h3>Chicken Drumsticks</h3><p>Medium heat, turn every 5 minutes: total <strong>30\u201335 minutes</strong>. Drumsticks cook unevenly due to their tapered shape, so frequent turning is more important than with other cuts.</p><h3>Whole Chicken (Spatchcocked)</h3><p>A spatchcocked (butterflied) chicken grills in <strong>35\u201345 minutes</strong> over indirect medium heat versus 75\u201390 minutes for a whole bird. This is the best method for even grilling of a whole chicken.</p><h2>The Resting Rule</h2><p>Rest grilled chicken for <strong>5 minutes</strong> after removing from heat. Cover loosely with foil. Juices redistribute significantly during this rest, and the internal temperature continues to rise 3\u20135\u00b0F from carryover cooking.</p>",
