            "id": "g_roast_vegetables_timer",
            "slug": "how-long-to-roast-vegetables",
            "title": "How Long to Roast Vegetables? Temperature and Timing Guide",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Roasting vegetables at the right temperature and for the right duration produces caramelized, tender results. This guide gives specific times for every common vegetable.",
            "content": "<h2>The Science of Roasting Vegetables</h2><p>Roasting uses high dry heat to accomplish two goals simultaneously: evaporating surface moisture (to prevent steaming) and triggering Maillard browning and caramelization of natural sugars. This is why roasted vegetables taste dramatically different from steamed or boiled ones \u2014 the concentrated, sweet, caramelized flavor comes from water loss and browning reactions that only occur at high heat.</p><h2>The Golden Rule of Vegetable Roasting</h2><p>Use <strong>400\u2013425\u00b0F (205\u2013220\u00b0C)</strong> for most vegetables. This temperature range drives the Maillard reaction without burning before the interior cooks through. Lower temperatures (350\u00b0F) produce less browning; higher temperatures (450\u00b0F) risk burning before softening, especially for dense vegetables.</p><h2>Roasting Times by Vegetable at 400\u00b0F</h2><h3>Dense, Starchy Vegetables (Longest)</h3><ul><li>Whole beets: <strong>45\u201360 minutes</strong> (wrapped in foil)</li><li>Butternut squash cubes (1 inch): <strong>25\u201335 minutes</strong></li><li>Sweet potato cubes (1 inch): <strong>25\u201330 minutes</strong></li><li>Carrots, halved or large rounds: <strong>20\u201330 minutes</strong></li><li>Whole garlic cloves: <strong>25\u201335 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Medium-Density Vegetables</h3><ul><li>Brussels sprouts, halved: <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li><li>Cauliflower florets: <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li><li>Broccoli florets: <strong>15\u201320 minutes</strong></li><li>Red onion wedges: <strong>25\u201330 minutes</strong></li><li>Whole mushrooms: <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Tender Vegetables (Shortest)</h3><ul><li>Cherry tomatoes: <strong>15\u201320 minutes</strong></li><li>Asparagus spears: <strong>10\u201315 minutes</strong></li><li>Zucchini rounds (1/2 inch): <strong>15\u201320 minutes</strong></li><li>Green beans: <strong>15\u201320 minutes</strong></li><li>Thinly sliced peppers: <strong>12\u201315 minutes</strong></li></ul><h2>Key Principles for Roasting Success</h2><p><strong>Do not crowd the pan.</strong> Vegetables touching each other steam instead of roast. Use one large sheet pan or two pans to spread vegetables in a single layer.</p><p><strong>Cut to uniform size.</strong> Uneven pieces cook unevenly. A 1-inch cube is a reliable standard for most root vegetables.</p><p><strong>Toss with oil.</strong> 1\u20132 tablespoons of oil per pound of vegetables is sufficient. More oil creates soggy results.</p><h2>The Flip Point</h2><p>Flip vegetables halfway through the roasting time to ensure even browning on two sides. For a 25-minute roasting time, flip at 12\u201313 minutes.</p>",
