            "excerpt": "Tea steeping time affects flavor, strength, and bitterness. This guide gives precise steeping times and water temperatures for every major tea type.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Steeping Time Matters</h2><p>Tea infusion is an extraction process. Steeping too briefly produces thin, weak tea lacking depth. Steeping too long extracts excess tannins and polyphenols that create astringency and bitterness. The optimal window extracts the flavor compounds you want while minimizing the bitter ones. This window varies dramatically by tea type because different processing methods produce different chemical compositions.</p><h2>Steeping Time by Tea Type</h2><h3>Green Tea</h3><p>Green tea is the most sensitive to oversteeping. Steeping for even 30 seconds too long can produce noticeable bitterness.</p><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>160\u2013175\u00b0F (70\u201380\u00b0C)</strong> \u2014 never use boiling water with green tea</li><li>Steeping time: <strong>1\u20133 minutes</strong></li><li>Start with 2 minutes for most green teas and adjust to taste</li></ul><h3>White Tea</h3><p>White tea is the least processed and most delicate. It can handle slightly longer steeps without bitterness.</p><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>160\u2013175\u00b0F (70\u201380\u00b0C)</strong></li><li>Steeping time: <strong>2\u20135 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Oolong Tea</h3><p>Oolong's steeping time varies based on oxidation level. Lighter oolongs (closer to green) use shorter times; darker oolongs (closer to black) use longer times.</p><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>185\u2013205\u00b0F (85\u201396\u00b0C)</strong></li><li>Steeping time: <strong>2\u20135 minutes</strong></li><li>Oolongs can often be re-steeped 3\u20135 times, adding 30 seconds per subsequent steep</li></ul><h3>Black Tea</h3><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>205\u2013212\u00b0F (96\u2013100\u00b0C)</strong> \u2014 near-boiling</li><li>Steeping time: <strong>3\u20135 minutes</strong></li><li>English Breakfast: 3\u20134 minutes; Assam: 3\u20135 minutes; Darjeeling: 2\u20133 minutes</li></ul><h3>Herbal Tea (Tisanes)</h3><p>Herbal teas are not true teas but are often steeped longer because most do not contain tannins that cause bitterness.</p><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>212\u00b0F (100\u00b0C)</strong> \u2014 full boiling</li><li>Steeping time: <strong>5\u201310 minutes</strong> for most herbals</li><li>Chamomile: 5 minutes; Peppermint: 7 minutes; Rooibos: 5\u20137 minutes</li></ul><h3>Pu-erh Tea</h3><ul><li>Water temperature: <strong>212\u00b0F (100\u00b0C)</strong></li><li>Steeping time: <strong>2\u20134 minutes</strong> (can be re-steeped many times)</li></ul><h2>Cold Brew Tea</h2><p>Cold brew tea steeps at room temperature or in the refrigerator for <strong>6\u201312 hours</strong>. This extraction method produces a naturally sweeter, less astringent cup because cold water extracts fewer tannins than hot water.</p>",
