            "excerpt": "Pasta cooking time varies by shape, size, and whether it is fresh or dried. This guide gives exact timing for al dente pasta for every common shape.",
            "content": "<h2>What Is Al Dente and Why Does It Matter?</h2><p>Al dente (Italian for 'to the tooth') describes pasta that is cooked through but still has a slight resistance when bitten \u2014 it should not be mushy or hard. Properly cooked al dente pasta has a slightly chewy center that is white when cut through (a tiny raw starchy line). This texture holds sauces better and has a lower glycemic index than overcooked pasta.</p><h2>The Two-Phase Cooking Rule</h2><p>Start checking pasta for doneness <strong>2 minutes before</strong> the package minimum time. Pasta continues cooking after draining from carryover heat and finishes cooking in the sauce if you add it immediately to a warm sauce. Pull pasta when it is just slightly underdone from your target if you plan to finish it in sauce.</p><h2>Dried Pasta Timing at Rolling Boil</h2><h3>Long Pasta</h3><ul><li>Spaghetti: <strong>8\u201310 minutes</strong></li><li>Linguine: <strong>9\u201311 minutes</strong></li><li>Fettuccine: <strong>10\u201312 minutes</strong></li><li>Pappardelle: <strong>10\u201313 minutes</strong></li><li>Angel hair (capellini): <strong>4\u20135 minutes</strong> (most delicate; check constantly)</li></ul><h3>Short Pasta</h3><ul><li>Penne: <strong>10\u201311 minutes</strong></li><li>Rigatoni: <strong>11\u201313 minutes</strong></li><li>Fusilli / rotini: <strong>10\u201312 minutes</strong></li><li>Farfalle (bow tie): <strong>11\u201313 minutes</strong></li><li>Orecchiette: <strong>11\u201313 minutes</strong></li><li>Elbow macaroni: <strong>8\u201310 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Stuffed Pasta</h3><ul><li>Tortellini (dried): <strong>10\u201312 minutes</strong></li><li>Tortellini (refrigerated): <strong>3\u20135 minutes</strong></li><li>Ravioli (refrigerated): <strong>4\u20136 minutes</strong></li></ul><h2>Fresh Pasta Timing</h2><p>Fresh pasta cooks dramatically faster than dried because it has not been dried out. Most fresh pasta shapes cook in <strong>2\u20134 minutes</strong> in boiling salted water. Watch carefully \u2014 overcooking happens fast with fresh pasta.</p><h2>Critical Tips for Perfect Pasta</h2><ul><li>Use a large pot with plenty of water (4\u20136 quarts per pound of pasta)</li><li>Salt the water generously \u2014 it should taste like the sea</li><li>Do not add oil to the water \u2014 it coats the pasta and prevents sauce adhesion</li><li>Reserve 1 cup of pasta water before draining \u2014 starchy water is an emulsifier for sauces</li><li>Never rinse pasta after draining unless making a cold pasta salad</li></ul>",
