            "excerpt": "Pressure cookers reduce cooking times by 60-70% compared to conventional methods. This guide gives pressure cooker times for common recipes and how to convert standard times.",
            "content": "<h2>How Pressure Cooking Changes Time</h2><p>A pressure cooker seals steam inside, raising the boiling point of water from 212\u00b0F (100\u00b0C) to approximately 250\u00b0F (121\u00b0C) at 15 PSI. This higher temperature cooks food 2\u20133 times faster than conventional methods. Understanding this compression allows you to accurately convert any standard recipe to pressure cooker timing.</p><h2>The General Conversion Rule</h2><p>Conventional cooking time divided by 3 equals approximate pressure cooking time. A roast that takes 3 hours in the oven takes approximately 60 minutes under pressure. This is a rough starting point \u2014 dense, collagen-rich proteins (pot roast, oxtail) convert closer to 1/3 time; delicate foods convert less dramatically.</p><h2>Common Pressure Cooker Times at High Pressure (15 PSI)</h2><h3>Beans and Legumes</h3><ul><li>Dried chickpeas: <strong>35\u201340 minutes</strong> (no soaking needed)</li><li>Dried black beans: <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li><li>Dried lentils: <strong>8\u201310 minutes</strong></li><li>Dried kidney beans: <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Grains</h3><ul><li>Brown rice: <strong>22\u201325 minutes</strong> (vs. 45 minutes conventional)</li><li>White rice: <strong>3\u20135 minutes</strong></li><li>Steel-cut oats: <strong>10\u201312 minutes</strong></li><li>Risotto: <strong>7 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Meat</h3><ul><li>Chicken breast: <strong>8\u201310 minutes</strong></li><li>Chicken thighs (bone-in): <strong>12\u201315 minutes</strong></li><li>Beef chuck (1-inch cubes for stew): <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li><li>Pork shoulder: <strong>35\u201345 minutes</strong></li><li>Beef brisket: <strong>60\u201390 minutes</strong></li><li>Beef short ribs: <strong>45\u201355 minutes</strong></li></ul><h3>Vegetables</h3><ul><li>Beets (whole, medium): <strong>20\u201325 minutes</strong></li><li>Potatoes (quartered): <strong>8\u201310 minutes</strong></li><li>Butternut squash (cubed): <strong>5\u20138 minutes</strong></li><li>Artichokes: <strong>10\u201312 minutes</strong></li></ul><h2>Pressure Release Methods and Their Effect on Timing</h2><p>Natural pressure release (NPR): Allow the pot to depressurize on its own, which takes 10\u201330 minutes. During this time, food continues cooking. Use NPR for meat, beans, and grains.</p><p>Quick release (QR): Manually release steam immediately. Food stops cooking quickly. Use for vegetables and delicate foods. Remember to add NPR time to the total if comparing to quick-release recipes.</p>",
