            "id": "g_rice_timer",
            "slug": "rice-cooking-timer",
            "title": "Rice Cooking Timer: Perfect Timing for Every Variety of Rice",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Rice cooking time varies significantly by variety. This guide gives exact timing and water ratios for white, brown, basmati, jasmine, arborio, and other rice varieties.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Rice Timing Matters</h2><p>Rice cooking is not forgiving. Under-timed rice is crunchy and raw in the center. Over-timed rice becomes mushy and clumped. Unlike proteins where you can check for doneness, rice must be timed correctly because lifting the lid during cooking disrupts the steam that cooks the top layer.</p><h2>The Standard Method: Absorption Method</h2><p>Bring water (with a pinch of salt and optional butter/oil) to a boil in a pot. Add rice, stir once, reduce heat to the lowest setting, cover tightly, and cook for the specified time. Do not lift the lid during cooking. Remove from heat and let steam off-heat for 5\u201310 minutes before fluffing with a fork.</p><h2>Cooking Times by Rice Variety (Absorption Method)</h2><h3>White Rice Varieties</h3><ul><li>Long-grain white rice: <strong>18 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.5 (1 cup rice : 1.5 cups water)</li><li>Medium-grain white rice: <strong>15\u201318 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.4</li><li>Short-grain white rice (sushi rice): <strong>15 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.25</li><li>Basmati rice: <strong>18 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.5 (soak 30 min first for best results)</li><li>Jasmine rice: <strong>15\u201318 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.25</li></ul><h3>Brown Rice Varieties</h3><ul><li>Long-grain brown rice: <strong>40\u201345 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:2</li><li>Short-grain brown rice: <strong>35\u201340 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:2</li><li>Brown basmati: <strong>40\u201345 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:2</li></ul><h3>Specialty Rice</h3><ul><li>Arborio (risotto method, not absorption): <strong>18\u201320 minutes</strong> with constant stirring and gradual stock addition</li><li>Wild rice: <strong>45\u201355 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:3</li><li>Black rice: <strong>30\u201335 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:1.75</li><li>Red rice: <strong>40 minutes</strong> | Water ratio: 1:2</li></ul><h2>The Off-Heat Steam Period</h2><p>After the timer ends, remove the pot from heat and let it sit covered for <strong>5\u201310 minutes</strong>. This off-heat steaming period is critical \u2014 it allows residual moisture to absorb evenly and the bottom layer to release from the pot. Skipping this step produces unevenly cooked rice with wet spots and crunchy patches.</p><h2>Rice Cooker Timing</h2><p>If using a rice cooker, follow the machine's internal timing \u2014 it uses a thermostat to detect when water is absorbed and switches to keep-warm mode automatically. White rice takes 20\u201330 minutes; brown rice takes 40\u201360 minutes in most rice cookers.</p>",
