            "id": "g_slow_cooker_timer",
            "slug": "slow-cooker-timing-guide",
            "title": "Slow Cooker Timing Guide: Low vs High Heat and Conversion Tips",
            "cluster": "cooking",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Slow cookers use two heat settings that affect cooking time significantly. Learn how to convert conventional recipes to slow cooker times and avoid the most common timing mistakes.",
            "content": "<h2>How Slow Cookers Work</h2><p>A slow cooker (Crock-Pot) uses very low, sustained heat \u2014 typically 170\u00b0F on low and 280\u00b0F on high \u2014 to cook food over an extended period. The tight lid traps moisture, essentially braising everything cooked inside. This makes slow cookers ideal for tough, collagen-rich cuts that transform into tender, falling-apart meat over long cooking periods.</p><h2>Low vs. High Setting: What's the Difference?</h2><p>The low setting (170\u00b0F) cooks food gently over 6\u201310 hours. The high setting (280\u00b0F) cooks the same food in approximately half the time: 3\u20135 hours. The end result is similar, but low-and-slow cooking generally produces more tender meat and better-developed flavors because collagen breaks down more completely at lower temperatures over longer periods.</p><h2>Standard Slow Cooker Times by Food</h2><h3>Beef</h3><ul><li>Chuck roast (3\u20134 lbs): <strong>Low: 8\u201310 hours | High: 4\u20135 hours</strong></li><li>Beef stew (cubed): <strong>Low: 7\u20138 hours | High: 3\u20134 hours</strong></li><li>Brisket: <strong>Low: 8\u201310 hours | High: 4\u20136 hours</strong></li><li>Short ribs: <strong>Low: 7\u20138 hours | High: 3\u20134 hours</strong></li></ul><h3>Pork</h3><ul><li>Pork shoulder (pulled pork, 4\u20136 lbs): <strong>Low: 10\u201312 hours | High: 5\u20136 hours</strong></li><li>Pork tenderloin: <strong>Low: 4\u20135 hours | High: 2\u20133 hours</strong></li><li>Pork ribs: <strong>Low: 6\u20138 hours | High: 3\u20134 hours</strong></li></ul><h3>Chicken</h3><ul><li>Whole chicken (3\u20134 lbs): <strong>Low: 6\u20138 hours | High: 3\u20134 hours</strong></li><li>Chicken thighs (bone-in): <strong>Low: 5\u20136 hours | High: 2\u20133 hours</strong></li><li>Chicken breasts: <strong>Low: 4\u20135 hours | High: 2\u20132.5 hours</strong> (watch carefully; overcooks easily)</li></ul><h3>Vegetables and Legumes</h3><ul><li>Dried beans: <strong>Low: 8\u201310 hours | High: 4\u20135 hours</strong></li><li>Vegetable soups and stews: <strong>Low: 6\u20138 hours | High: 3\u20134 hours</strong></li></ul><h2>Converting Oven Recipes to Slow Cooker</h2><p>As a general rule: 1 hour in the oven at 350\u00b0F = 3\u20134 hours on high or 6\u20138 hours on low in a slow cooker. Reduce liquid in the recipe by about 30% because slow cookers retain moisture and do not allow evaporation.</p><h2>Common Slow Cooker Timing Mistakes</h2><ul><li>Overcooking chicken breasts (they become dry and stringy after 6+ hours on low)</li><li>Adding dairy or cream too early (add in the final 15\u201330 minutes)</li><li>Lifting the lid during cooking (each lift adds 15\u201330 minutes of cooking time)</li><li>Using high setting for all recipes (some foods, particularly fish, should never be slow cooked)</li></ul>",
