            "excerpt": "Sourdough timing is more complex than commercial yeast bread because fermentation speed depends on starter health and ambient temperature. This guide gives reliable proofing windows for every stage.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Sourdough Timing Is Different</h2><p>Commercial yeast bread uses a standardized product with predictable activity. Sourdough uses a live, wild yeast starter that varies in strength, hydration, and bacterial composition. This means sourdough timing is always a range \u2014 and learning to read dough rather than relying exclusively on the timer is essential. The timer keeps you in the ballpark; the dough tells you when it is ready.</p><h2>Stage 1: Levain Build (3\u201312 Hours)</h2><p>Before making the final dough, you feed your starter (levain) and let it peak. This takes <strong>4\u20138 hours at room temperature (70\u201375\u00b0F)</strong> or up to 12 hours in cooler conditions. Your levain is ready when it has doubled in volume, has a domed top (not fallen), and passes the float test: a teaspoon dropped in water floats rather than sinks.</p><h2>Stage 2: Autolyse (30\u201360 Minutes)</h2><p>Autolyse means combining flour and water and letting it rest before adding starter and salt. This resting period (typically <strong>30\u201360 minutes</strong>) allows the flour to fully hydrate and gluten to begin developing without mixing effort. It dramatically improves dough extensibility.</p><h2>Stage 3: Bulk Fermentation (4\u201312 Hours)</h2><p>After mixing the final dough (flour + water + levain + salt), the bulk fermentation begins. At <strong>75\u00b0F (24\u00b0C)</strong>, expect <strong>4\u20136 hours</strong>. At 70\u00b0F (21\u00b0C), expect <strong>6\u20138 hours</strong>. At 65\u00b0F (18\u00b0C), expect <strong>8\u201312 hours</strong>. The dough should increase by 50\u201375% and show active bubbles on the surface and sides.</p><h3>Stretch and Fold During Bulk</h3><p>During bulk fermentation, perform stretch-and-fold sets every <strong>30\u201345 minutes</strong> for the first 2\u20133 hours (4\u20136 sets total). Each set takes about 1 minute. This builds gluten strength without deflating fermentation gases.</p><h2>Stage 4: Shaping and Cold Proof (8\u201318 Hours)</h2><p>After bulk fermentation and shaping, most sourdough bakers do a cold proof (retard) in the refrigerator. This typically runs <strong>8\u201318 hours</strong> at 38\u201340\u00b0F (3\u20134\u00b0C). Longer cold proofs develop more sour flavor. The cold proof can be extended to 24 hours for most recipes without over-fermentation.</p><h2>Stage 5: Baking (40\u201350 Minutes)</h2><p>Preheat Dutch oven at 500\u00b0F (260\u00b0C) for 45\u201360 minutes. Bake covered for <strong>20 minutes</strong> (steam creates oven spring), then uncover and bake for <strong>20\u201325 more minutes</strong> until deep brown. Internal temperature should reach 205\u2013210\u00b0F (96\u201399\u00b0C).</p>",
