            "id": "g_spaced_rep_timer",
            "slug": "spaced-repetition-timer",
            "title": "Spaced Repetition Timer: Optimal Review Intervals for Long-Term Memory",
            "cluster": "study",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Spaced repetition is the most evidence-backed study technique for long-term memory. This guide explains the forgetting curve, review intervals, and how to use timers to implement spaced repetition manually or with software.",
            "content": "<h2>The Forgetting Curve</h2><p>Hermann Ebbinghaus discovered in the 1880s that memory decays exponentially without reinforcement. Within 24 hours of learning something, approximately 70% is forgotten. Within a week, 90%. However, if you review the material just before you are about to forget it, the memory trace is strengthened and the next forgetting curve is less steep. This is the principle behind spaced repetition.</p><h2>The Spacing Effect in Practice</h2><p>Reviewing a piece of information at increasing intervals \u2014 for example, 1 day, 3 days, 7 days, 14 days, 30 days \u2014 produces dramatically stronger long-term retention than cramming the same study time into a single session. Research consistently shows spaced practice produces 200\u2013400% better long-term retention than massed practice.</p><h2>Manual Spaced Repetition Schedule</h2><p>If using physical flashcards or notes rather than an app, use the Leitner box system:</p><ul><li>New material \u2192 Box 1 (review every day)</li><li>After correct recall \u2192 Box 2 (review every 2 days)</li><li>After correct recall \u2192 Box 3 (review every 4 days)</li><li>After correct recall \u2192 Box 4 (review every 8 days)</li><li>After correct recall \u2192 Box 5 (review every 16 days)</li></ul><p>Set calendar reminders or a recurring timer to trigger each box's review at the appropriate interval.</p><h2>Daily Spaced Repetition Session Timing</h2><p>A daily spaced repetition session should be timed at <strong>15\u201320 minutes</strong> to maintain sustainable habit formation. Anki and similar apps show you exactly which cards are due each day based on the algorithm. The timer ensures you review every due card in each session without letting the habit expand to the point where it becomes burdensome.</p><h2>Distributing Study vs. Massed Practice: The Data</h2><p>Studies comparing spaced practice to cramming consistently show that students who space review over days before a test outperform crammers on exams taken 1 week later by an average of 150%, and outperform by even larger margins on tests taken 1 month later. The short-term advantage of cramming is real but temporary; the long-term advantage of spacing is robust.</p><h2>Timer Settings for Spaced Repetition</h2><p>Set a daily reminder at a consistent time (morning or evening). Run a 20-minute countdown during your Anki/flashcard session. Use the app's own scheduler for what to review \u2014 do not override it with judgment about what feels important. Trust the algorithm.</p>",
