            "id": "g_time_blocking_timer",
            "slug": "time-blocking-timer",
            "title": "Time Blocking Timer: Schedule Your Day in Focused Blocks",
            "cluster": "productivity",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Time blocking assigns specific tasks to fixed calendar blocks to prevent reactive work from crowding out meaningful output. Use these timer setups to implement time blocking effectively.",
            "content": "<h2>What Is Time Blocking?</h2><p>Time blocking is the practice of scheduling your work day into pre-defined time blocks, each assigned to a specific task or task category. Rather than working from a to-do list in an undefined time frame, you assign each task a specific time slot in your calendar. This converts intentions into commitments and eliminates the ambiguity that allows reactive work to dominate the day.</p><h2>Time Blocking vs. Pomodoro: Key Differences</h2><p>Pomodoro uses fixed 25-minute intervals regardless of task type. Time blocking is more flexible \u2014 blocks can be 30 minutes, 90 minutes, 2 hours, or any duration that fits the task. Time blocking at the daily planning level; Pomodoro at the micro-execution level. Many practitioners use both: time blocking sets the structure for the day, Pomodoro drives focus within each block.</p><h2>Standard Time Block Lengths</h2><h3>Deep Work Blocks: 90\u2013120 Minutes</h3><p>Schedule your most cognitively demanding tasks in 90\u2013120 minute blocks during your peak energy hours (typically morning for most people). Set a timer at the start of each block. During this block, no communication, no task switching.</p><h3>Administrative Blocks: 30\u201360 Minutes</h3><p>Email, calendar management, and administrative tasks go in dedicated 30\u201360 minute blocks, typically mid-morning and late afternoon. Having a defined block for these tasks prevents them from expanding throughout the day.</p><h3>Meeting Blocks: As Scheduled</h3><p>Group meetings into adjacent time blocks when possible to preserve long uninterrupted stretches for deep work. Back-to-back meetings in a 2\u20133 hour window is better than scattered 1-hour meetings throughout the day.</p><h2>How to Time Block Your Day</h2><ol><li>List all tasks that need to be done today</li><li>Categorize tasks by type and energy requirement</li><li>Assign each task to a time block based on energy match (demanding tasks when you are most alert)</li><li>Set a timer at the start of each block</li><li>Work exclusively on the assigned task until the block ends</li><li>At block end, assess completion and adjust remaining plan</li></ol><h2>Dealing with Interruptions in Time Blocking</h2><p>Interruptions happen. When they do, note the interruption, handle it if genuinely urgent, and return to your block. Reset the timer if significant time was lost. At the end of the day, note how many interruptions occurred and what caused them \u2014 this data helps you protect future blocks more effectively.</p>",
