            "id": "g_meeting_timer",
            "slug": "meeting-timer-guide",
            "title": "Meeting Timer Guide: Keep Meetings Short and Productive",
            "cluster": "productivity",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Most meetings run too long and accomplish too little. This guide gives timer-based structures for keeping meetings focused, on-agenda, and ending on time.",
            "content": "<h2>The Meeting Length Problem</h2><p>Parkinson's Law states that work expands to fill the time available. Nowhere is this more observable than in meetings: a 60-minute meeting slot fills 60 minutes regardless of whether the actual agenda requires 60 minutes. Using a meeting timer creates artificial time pressure that forces prioritization, reduces tangential discussion, and respects participants' time.</p><h2>Recommended Meeting Lengths by Type</h2><h3>Status Updates: 15 Minutes</h3><p>Stand-up meetings and status updates have no business being longer than 15 minutes. Set a visible countdown timer at the start of the meeting. Each participant's update is time-boxed to 2\u20133 minutes. The timer creates social pressure to be concise.</p><h3>Decision-Making Meetings: 30\u201345 Minutes</h3><p>Meetings with a clear decision to be made should have a 30\u201345 minute limit. Structure: 5 minutes framing the decision, 20\u201330 minutes of discussion, 5 minutes deciding. The decision-focused structure prevents meetings from becoming discussion forums that never reach conclusions.</p><h3>Brainstorming Sessions: 45\u201360 Minutes</h3><p>Creative brainstorming benefits from a defined end point that creates urgency. A 45-minute brainstorming session with a visible countdown produces more novel ideas than an open-ended session that drifts toward consensus and risk-aversion as time passes.</p><h3>Weekly Team Check-Ins: 30 Minutes</h3><p>Regular team meetings can be conducted in 30 minutes with agenda discipline. Distribute the agenda 24 hours in advance. Start on time (do not wait for latecomers). Use a 30-minute visible timer. End when the timer ends, even if not everything is covered \u2014 remaining items move to next week or to asynchronous communication.</p><h2>The Time-Box Agenda</h2><p>Before the meeting, assign a time box to each agenda item. If you have 3 items in a 45-minute meeting, each item might get 12 minutes with 9 minutes of buffer. Set a timer per agenda item, not just a meeting-end timer. When an agenda item's timer ends, move to the next item whether the previous is resolved or not. Unresolved items are followed up asynchronously.</p><h2>Facilitation Timer Techniques</h2><p>As a facilitator, use a visible shared timer (screen-shared countdown in virtual meetings, physical timer on the table in person) to give the group a collective sense of time. Announce when there are 5 minutes remaining in the meeting to allow for decision-crystallization and next-steps assignment. End exactly on time to build reputation for meeting discipline.</p>",
