            "cluster": "study",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Effective exam preparation requires structured study sessions across multiple days. This guide gives timer-based study schedules from 1 week to 1 month before exams.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Timed Exam Prep Outperforms Marathon Sessions</h2><p>Cramming \u2014 studying all material in one or two marathon sessions close to the exam \u2014 is the single least effective study strategy identified by cognitive psychology research. Students who cram perform similarly on the next-day exam but retain almost nothing one week later. Distributed practice using timed sessions over days or weeks produces dramatically better long-term retention and better exam performance on tests that require application rather than simple recall.</p><h2>One Week to Exam: Daily Study Schedule</h2><p>With one week remaining, structure daily study sessions as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Day 7\u20136 (6\u20135 days out)</strong>: Comprehensive review \u2014 cover all material at surface level. Set three 50-minute study blocks with 10-minute breaks. Total study time: 2.5 hours per day.</li><li><strong>Day 5\u20134</strong>: Targeted review \u2014 identify weak areas from initial review. Focus 70% of study time on those areas. Same block structure.</li><li><strong>Day 3\u20132</strong>: Practice tests and self-testing. Spend 90 minutes on practice questions or past exam papers. Review incorrect answers for 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Day 1 (day before)</strong>: Light review only. Set a single 45-minute timer for the most difficult concepts. No new material. Sleep is the priority.</li></ul><h2>One Month to Exam: Weekly Structure</h2><p>With four weeks remaining, spread content review over 3 weeks with a final review week:</p><ul><li><strong>Week 1</strong>: Content area 1 \u2014 5 study sessions of 60 minutes each</li><li><strong>Week 2</strong>: Content area 2 \u2014 5 study sessions of 60 minutes each</li><li><strong>Week 3</strong>: Content area 3 + weak areas from weeks 1\u20132</li><li><strong>Week 4</strong>: Integrated review + practice tests + spaced repetition of all material</li></ul><h2>Timer Settings for Practice Exams</h2><p>Simulate real exam conditions by timing practice tests accurately. If the real exam is 2 hours, set a 2-hour timer and do not pause it. This trains test-taking pacing skills \u2014 knowing when to move on from stuck questions and when to allocate more time to complex problems. Most test anxiety stems partly from feeling unable to manage time during the exam; timed practice sessions build confidence in time management under pressure.</p><h2>The Night Before: Timer Discipline</h2><p>Set a stop-studying alarm for the night before the exam. No studying after 8 PM. Use an evening meditation or light relaxation session (15\u201320 minutes) instead. Sleep consolidates the memories formed during study sessions \u2014 8 hours of sleep before an exam is more valuable than 8 hours of additional studying the night before.</p>",
