            "slug": "how-long-to-nap",
            "title": "How Long Should You Nap? The Science of Power Nap Timing",
            "cluster": "study",
            "primaryHub": "hub_minutes",
            "excerpt": "Nap length dramatically affects whether you wake refreshed or groggy. This guide gives the science-backed duration for power naps, memory-enhancing naps, and long recovery naps.",
            "content": "<h2>Why Nap Duration Is Critical</h2><p>A 10-minute nap and a 30-minute nap produce entirely different physiological outcomes. The difference comes down to sleep stages: brief naps stay in light sleep (Stage 1 and 2), while longer naps enter slow-wave sleep (Stage 3). Waking from slow-wave sleep causes sleep inertia \u2014 the groggy, disoriented feeling that can last 30\u201360 minutes after waking. Getting nap duration right means maximizing the restorative benefit without the sleep inertia penalty.</p><h2>The 10\u201320 Minute Power Nap</h2><p>The power nap is the most universally recommended nap duration for working adults and students. Ten to 20 minutes keeps you in Stage 1 and 2 sleep \u2014 light sleep that restores alertness, improves mood, and enhances performance without sleep inertia. You wake feeling refreshed rather than groggy.</p><p>NASA research found that a 26-minute nap improved pilot performance by 34% and alertness by 100%. Set your timer for 20 minutes from when you close your eyes (not from when you fall asleep, since you may take 5\u201310 minutes to drift off).</p><h2>The 30-Minute Nap: Proceed with Caution</h2><p>A 30-minute nap crosses into slow-wave sleep for many people, particularly those who are sleep-deprived. The result can be significant sleep inertia lasting 20\u201330 minutes after waking \u2014 a period of impaired alertness that cancels the benefit. If you regularly experience post-nap grogginess, shorten your nap to 20 minutes or extend it to 90 minutes (see below).</p><h2>The 90-Minute Full Cycle Nap</h2><p>A 90-minute nap allows you to complete one full sleep cycle (NREM \u2192 slow-wave \u2192 REM). You wake at the natural end of a cycle, which minimizes sleep inertia. Ninety-minute naps improve emotional processing, procedural memory (motor skills), and creative problem-solving through REM sleep. Use a 90-minute timer and set it only when you have the time and privacy to dedicate.</p><h2>The Caffeine Nap (Nappuccino)</h2><p>The caffeine nap exploits the timing of caffeine absorption: drink coffee immediately before taking a 20-minute nap. Caffeine takes approximately 20\u201330 minutes to be absorbed and reach peak blood concentration. By the time you wake from the nap, both the natural restorative benefit of the nap and the caffeine effect are peaking simultaneously. Research shows caffeine naps outperform either intervention alone.</p><h2>Best Time to Nap</h2><p>The ideal nap window is <strong>1\u20133 PM</strong>, which aligns with the natural post-lunch dip in the circadian alertness curve. Napping after 4 PM risks interfering with nighttime sleep onset, particularly for people with sleep difficulties.</p>",
