            "slug": "how-long-is-a-pomodoro",
            "title": "How Long Is a Pomodoro? Understanding the Classic 25-Minute Session",
            "cluster": "pomodoro",
            "primaryHub": "hub_pomodoro",
            "excerpt": "A Pomodoro is 25 minutes, but why 25? This guide explains the history, science, and logic behind the original Pomodoro interval, plus when to deviate from it.",
            "content": "<h2>The Original Pomodoro: 25 Minutes</h2><p>A Pomodoro is precisely <strong>25 minutes of focused work followed by a 5-minute break</strong>. The name comes from the Italian word for tomato \u2014 Francesco Cirillo, who developed the technique in the late 1980s as a university student, used a tomato-shaped kitchen timer to time his study sessions. The 25-minute interval was not derived from neuroscience research; it was the duration that felt natural and productive to Cirillo during his own experiments.</p><h2>Why 25 Minutes Became the Standard</h2><p>The 25-minute interval caught on because it represents a meaningful chunk of focused work without feeling overwhelming. It is short enough that beginning feels manageable (reducing procrastination) and long enough to make real progress on most tasks. It also produces a natural 4-per-hour rhythm with breaks, which many people find sustainable across a full work day.</p><h2>The Full Pomodoro Cycle</h2><p>One Pomodoro is not just 25 minutes \u2014 it is a 30-minute cycle:</p><ul><li>25 minutes: Focused work</li><li>5 minutes: Short break</li></ul><p>After 4 complete Pomodoros (2 hours of work with breaks), the technique calls for a longer break of 15\u201330 minutes before the next set of 4 begins. This longer break is essential for recovery between Pomodoro sets.</p><h2>When to Deviate from 25 Minutes</h2><p>The 25-minute Pomodoro is a starting point, not a law. Many practitioners find that it works poorly for certain task types:</p><ul><li><strong>Complex deep work</strong>: 25 minutes may feel disruptive at exactly the point when flow state begins. Consider 45 or 50-minute sessions instead.</li><li><strong>ADHD or attention challenges</strong>: 25 minutes may feel impossibly long. Start with 10 or 15-minute Pomodoros and work up.</li><li><strong>Highly repetitive tasks</strong>: 25 minutes is usually fine for mechanical or administrative work.</li></ul><h2>The 4-Pomodoro Set: One Work Block</h2><p>A set of 4 Pomodoros represents approximately 2 hours of productive work time (100 minutes of focus plus 20 minutes of breaks). This is a natural morning or afternoon work block. After 4 Pomodoros and the long break, starting a new set is common. Most practitioners complete 6\u20138 Pomodoros (150\u2013200 minutes of focus) in a productive workday.</p><h2>Tracking Pomodoros</h2><p>Cirillo's original method involved making a mark on paper for each completed Pomodoro. This simple tracking creates a visual record of daily focus output and provides a gamification element that many practitioners find motivating. Even minimal tracking \u2014 a tally of completed Pomodoros \u2014 provides useful data about your productive capacity.</p>",
